//! member: true
//!
//! std.signal — what a signal does to this process, and hearing one as an
//! event. Stability tier: core. The facade over wolf-lang's
//! `[os.signal]` family (s114, s188, s219, s232).
//!
//! **A signal is named by its MEANING, never its number** (`[os.signal.set]`):
//! `reload()` is `SIGHUP`, `interrupt()` is the terminal's Ctrl-C, and a
//! set is the bitwise OR of meanings — `signal.interrupt() | signal.quit()`.
//! The meanings are functions here because a module of the library
//! carries no constants; each answers its bit. Twenty are LISTENABLE —
//! every signal POSIX names that a program may catch (`reload()` …
//! `file_limit()`), the ones a set may hold. Eight more are SEND-ONLY
//! (`kill()`, `stop()` and the six fault signals): `send` and `number`
//! take them and nothing else does.
//!
//! **A meaning's bit never changes, and a program never writes one**
//! (`[os.signal.set.stable]`). Every meaning a release has published
//! keeps its bit forever — it is never reassigned or reused, even if the
//! meaning is retired — and a new meaning takes the lowest bit no release
//! has published. So a set stored by one release means the same to the
//! next, and the only spelling of a meaning that is portable is its NAME:
//! call the function, never write the bit and never a host's signal
//! number. `number` is the one way from a meaning to the number this
//! host gives it (`USER` is 10 on linux and 30 on macOS), for the places
//! a number is the interface — `kill -l`, `trap … 13`, `128 + N`.
//!
//! **Three dispositions, nothing else.** A meaning's signal either does
//! what the host does by default (`reset`), nothing (`ignore`), or arrives
//! as an event (`listen`, then `wait` or `poll`). There is no handler: no
//! wolf code ever runs when a signal arrives (`[os.signal.model]`). The
//! last call for a meaning wins.
//!
//! **What a child inherits** is what `exec` leaves (`[os.signal.disp]`): an
//! ignored meaning stays ignored in every child, a listened one is the
//! default there. That is why a shell LISTENS for `interrupt()` rather than
//! ignoring it — it survives Ctrl-C and its `sleep` still dies of it — and
//! why `std.process.start_job` takes the meanings to set back to default in
//! a child the shell ignores for itself.
//!
//! **Errors are rows, and one shape rule reads every set** (s232). An
//! empty set, or one holding any bit that is not a listenable meaning, is
//! `io` from `listen`, `wait`, `wait_fd`, `poll`, `ignore` and `reset`,
//! and nothing changes; `raise` takes exactly one listenable meaning. On
//! windows only the first four meanings can be listened for — the
//! console sends `terminate()` and `quit()`, and `reload()` and
//! `upgrade()` arrive only by this process's own `raise` — so a listen
//! for any of the other sixteen is `io` there, and `ignore`, `reset`,
//! `ignored`, `send`, `number`, `wait_fd`, `send_group`, `send_all`,
//! `text`, `last` and `realtime` answer `unsupported`, `send` to `text`
//! after their own shape (`[os.signal.platform]`).
//!
//! **Groups and host numbers (s241, ruling #79).** `send` reaches one
//! process; `send_group` reaches every member of a `std.process.Group`
//! (a job), and `send_all` every process this one may signal, each by
//! its own name so that no miscomputed pid becomes a group or a
//! broadcast. A signal the table gives no meaning (the real-time range,
//! linux `IO` and `PWR`, macOS `INFO`) is sent by its host number,
//! `host(n)`, with `last()` and `realtime()` saying which numbers this
//! host has; `text` is a signal's words. The rows are the host's, kept
//! rather than evened out (R3).
//!
//! **Capability: `exec` (I13)** — signal control is process control:
//! `listen`, `wait`, `wait_fd`, `poll`, `ignore`, `reset`, `raise`,
//! `send`, `send_group` and `send_all` are tagged `exec` in the sandbox
//! table. `ignored` READS this process's context and is `env` (ruling
//! #70 R8, with `os_pgid`); `number`, `last`, `realtime` and `text` read
//! the host's table and charge nothing (ruling #79 R6). All of them are
//! refused at comptime (D33). The meaning functions and `host` reach
//! nothing and are comptime-safe.

use std.fs
use std.process

// THE TABLE (`[os.signal.set]`). These functions are the one place in
// `std/` where a signal's bit is written, and each bit is permanent
// (`[os.signal.set.stable]`): a line here is never edited, only added
// to, and a new meaning takes the lowest bit no release has published.
// Everything else in the library, and every program, names a meaning by
// calling its function.

/// Re-read configuration (`SIGHUP`).
pub fn reload() -> int { 1 }

/// Shut down now (`SIGTERM`).
pub fn terminate() -> int { 2 }

/// Shut down after draining (`SIGQUIT`; the terminal's Ctrl-\).
pub fn quit() -> int { 4 }

/// Swap in a new binary (`SIGUSR2`).
pub fn upgrade() -> int { 8 }

/// The terminal's interrupt character, Ctrl-C (`SIGINT`).
pub fn interrupt() -> int { 16 }

/// The terminal's suspend character, Ctrl-Z (`SIGTSTP`).
pub fn suspend() -> int { 32 }

/// A background process group read the terminal (`SIGTTIN`).
pub fn bg_read() -> int { 64 }

/// A background process group wrote to, or set, the terminal (`SIGTTOU`).
pub fn bg_write() -> int { 128 }

/// A child stopped or ended (`SIGCHLD`).
pub fn child() -> int { 256 }

/// Another program's own signal (`SIGUSR1`; `upgrade()` is `SIGUSR2`).
pub fn user() -> int { 512 }

/// A timer ran out (`SIGALRM`).
pub fn alarm() -> int { 1024 }

/// A write to a pipe or socket nobody reads (`SIGPIPE`). Ignored, the
/// write is an `io` row instead of this process's end, and every child
/// starts with it ignored too (`[os.signal.disp]`).
pub fn broken_pipe() -> int { 2048 }

/// The process was asked to abort (`SIGABRT`).
pub fn abort() -> int { 4096 }

/// The process was continued after a stop (`SIGCONT`). The table's name
/// is `CONTINUE`; `continue` is a keyword, so the function says what
/// happened.
pub fn continued() -> int { 8192 }

/// The terminal's window changed size (`SIGWINCH`).
pub fn resize() -> int { 16384 }

/// Out-of-band data arrived on a socket (`SIGURG`).
pub fn urgent() -> int { 32768 }

/// A user-time timer ran out (`SIGVTALRM`).
pub fn virtual_alarm() -> int { 65536 }

/// A profiling timer ran out (`SIGPROF`).
pub fn profile_alarm() -> int { 131072 }

/// The CPU-time limit was passed (`SIGXCPU`).
pub fn cpu_limit() -> int { 262144 }

/// The file-size limit was passed (`SIGXFSZ`).
pub fn file_limit() -> int { 524288 }

// The eight meanings below may only be SENT (`send`) and asked the
// number of (`number`): no process can catch or ignore the first two,
// and a program that returned from a real fault would run it again. A
// set that holds one is `io` from every set-taking call.

/// End the process now; it cannot be caught or ignored (`SIGKILL`).
/// Send-only.
pub fn kill() -> int { 1048576 }

/// Stop the process; it cannot be caught or ignored (`SIGSTOP`).
/// Send-only; `continued()` is what starts it again.
pub fn stop() -> int { 2097152 }

/// An invalid memory reference (`SIGSEGV`). Send-only.
pub fn segment_fault() -> int { 4194304 }

/// An access to an undefined part of a memory object (`SIGBUS`).
/// Send-only.
pub fn bus_error() -> int { 8388608 }

/// An erroneous arithmetic operation (`SIGFPE`). Send-only.
pub fn arithmetic() -> int { 16777216 }

/// An illegal instruction (`SIGILL`). Send-only.
pub fn illegal() -> int { 33554432 }

/// A trace or breakpoint trap (`SIGTRAP`). Send-only.
pub fn breakpoint() -> int { 67108864 }

/// A bad system call (`SIGSYS`). Send-only.
pub fn bad_syscall() -> int { 134217728 }

/// Hear every meaning in `set` as an event (`os_signal_listen`); ends an
/// ignore of them.
pub fn listen(set: int) -> () ! {io} {
    os_signal_listen(set)?
}

/// Park until a meaning in `set` arrives; that meaning.
pub fn wait(set: int) -> int ! {io} {
    os_signal_wait(set)?
}

/// Wait until the file `f` names has something to read or a meaning in
/// `set` is delivered (`[os.signal.wait.fd]`): the meaning, taken off
/// the queue as `wait` takes it, or **0** when `f` is ready — a byte,
/// the end of input or a hang-up — with nothing read, so the read that
/// follows says which. A meaning wins when both are ready; one delivered
/// or pending before the call is answered at once. The meanings must be
/// listened for first (`listen`). Any readable descriptor serves: a
/// terminal, a pipe, a file (spelling ruled #82, the family's).
///
/// This is the wait an interactive program blocks in instead of a read:
/// a delivered signal never tears a read, so an editor reading keys
/// would learn of a resize or a `kill` only when the next key came. It
/// has no deadline; on a terminal with `canonical` off, `std.term`'s
/// `Mode.min` and `Mode.time` time the read that follows.
///
/// Rows: `io` for a set the shape rule refuses — empty, or holding a
/// bit that is not a listenable meaning — before anything else; then
/// `unsupported` on windows; then `io` for a closed or forged handle.
/// Never traps. lupin answers the shape rows and declines a well-formed
/// call by name (it has no `poll`).
///
/// Capability `exec` (signal control, with `wait`); refused at comptime
/// (D33). Delegates to `os_signal_wait_fd`. The example is prose, since
/// a handle comes from `std.fs`: an editor listens for
/// `signal.resize()`, then loops on `signal.wait_fd(tty, set)?`,
/// redrawing on a meaning and reading a key on 0.
pub fn wait_fd(f: fs.File, set: int) -> int ! {unsupported, io} {
    os_signal_wait_fd(f.fd, set)?
}

/// The oldest meaning in `set` that has arrived, taken; 0 when none has.
/// Never waits (`[os.signal.poll]`).
pub fn poll(set: int) -> int ! {io} {
    os_signal_poll(set)?
}

/// Every meaning in `set` does nothing to this process — and to every
/// child it starts (`[os.signal.disp]`).
pub fn ignore(set: int) -> () ! {unsupported, io} {
    os_signal_ignore(set)?
}

/// Every meaning in `set` does what the host does by default.
pub fn reset(set: int) -> () ! {unsupported, io} {
    os_signal_default(set)?
}

/// Send `meaning` to this process (`os_signal_raise`).
pub fn raise(meaning: int) -> () ! {io} {
    os_signal_raise(meaning)?
}

/// **s232 (`[os.signal.send]`): send `meaning`'s signal to the process
/// `pid`** — `kill(2)` for one process. `meaning` is one meaning of the
/// table, a host number (`host(n)`, from wolf 0.2.31: `-n` was `invalid`
/// before), or 0, which sends nothing and answers the same rows: "is
/// there such a process, and may I signal it". A process id names whichever
/// process holds it NOW (a child already reaped may have passed its id
/// on); `std.process.kill` on a `Child` has no such hazard. A send to
/// this process's own id is a process-directed `raise`.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `invalid` when `pid` is not
/// above 0 or does not fit a process id, or `meaning` is neither 0, nor
/// exactly one meaning, nor `host(n)` with `1 <= n <= last()` (nothing
/// is sent); `unsupported` on a host with
/// no such call (windows, by name); then the target's — `not_found`
/// when no process has that id, `denied` when this process may not
/// signal it, `io` otherwise. Never traps. lupin answers the shape and
/// declines the send itself by name (no host call without `unsafe`).
///
/// Capability `exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `os_signal_send`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var row = "sent"
/// signal.send(0, signal.terminate()) else |_| { row = "refused" }
/// row == "refused"
/// ```
pub fn send(pid: int, meaning: int) -> () ! {unsupported, invalid, not_found, denied, io} {
    os_signal_send(pid, meaning)?
}

/// **s241 (`[os.signal.send.group]`, ruling #79 R1): send `meaning`'s
/// signal to every member of the process group `g`** that this process
/// may signal — `kill(-pgid)`, what `kill %1`, `fg`, `bg` and `kill --
/// -N` do to a job. `meaning` is one meaning of the table, a host number
/// (`host(n)`), or 0, which sends nothing and asks the group the same
/// question. A `STOP` stops every member and a `continued()` continues
/// them; the parent hears each member's stop through `std.process.poll`.
/// `kill 0` is `send_group(process.own_group()?, m)`, and it reaches this
/// process too.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `invalid` for a group id not
/// above 1 or wider than a process id — group 1 cannot be named, so a
/// miscomputed id never becomes a broadcast (`send_all` is that, by name)
/// — or a `meaning` `send` refuses; `unsupported` on windows; then the
/// host's answer, kept as it is (ruling #79 R3): `not_found` for a group
/// with no member, `denied` when this process may signal none of them,
/// `io` otherwise. A group some of whose members may be signalled is ok
/// and reaches those only; a group of ended, unreaped members is ok on
/// linux and `denied` on macOS. Never traps. lupin answers the shape and
/// declines the send itself by name.
///
/// Capability `exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `os_signal_send_group`. The example is prose, since a doc example
/// imports only this module: `signal.send_group(process.Group { id: 1 },
/// signal.terminate())` is `invalid`.
pub fn send_group(g: process.Group, meaning: int) -> () ! {
    unsupported,
    invalid,
    not_found,
    denied,
    io,
} {
    os_signal_send_group(g.id, meaning)?
}

/// **s241 (`[os.signal.send.all]`, ruling #79 R1): send `meaning`'s
/// signal to every process this one may signal**, itself excepted —
/// `kill(-1)`, XCU `kill -1`. Which processes that is is the host's rule:
/// as the super-user every process but this one (and, on linux, its pid
/// namespace's process 1); as another user, that user's processes in any
/// session or group. It has a name of its own so that no miscomputed pid
/// or group becomes it.
///
/// Rows: `invalid` for a `meaning` `send` refuses; `unsupported` on
/// windows; then the host's answer as it is: on linux `ok` when it found
/// any other process, even only ones it may not signal, and `not_found`
/// when it found none; `denied` where a host says so; `io` otherwise.
/// Never traps. lupin answers the shape and declines the send by name.
///
/// Capability `exec`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to
/// `os_signal_send_all`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var row = "sent"
/// signal.send_all(signal.terminate() | signal.user()) else |_| { row = "refused" }
/// row == "refused"
/// ```
pub fn send_all(meaning: int) -> () ! {unsupported, invalid, not_found, denied, io} {
    os_signal_send_all(meaning)?
}

/// **s232 (`[os.signal.number]`): the number THIS host gives `meaning`**
/// — what `kill -l` prints, what `trap … 13` names and what a shell's
/// `128 + N` decodes. It differs between hosts, which is why nothing
/// else in this module takes or answers one.
///
/// Rows: `invalid` for anything but exactly one meaning of the table;
/// `unsupported` on a host that does not number signals (windows).
/// Never traps.
///
/// Carries no capability (it reads the host's numbering and nothing
/// else), and is refused at comptime all the same: the compiling host's
/// number is not the running one's (D33). Delegates to
/// `os_signal_number`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let n = signal.number(signal.interrupt()) else |_| 1
/// n >= 1
/// let none = signal.number(0) else |_| 0 - 1
/// none == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn number(meaning: int) -> int ! {invalid, unsupported} {
    os_signal_number(meaning)?
}

/// **s241 (`[os.signal.host]`, ruling #79 R4): this host's last signal
/// number** — the C library's `SIGRTMAX` on linux (64 under glibc and
/// musl), 31 on macOS. A host number `N` from 1 to this is what `host(N)`
/// spells and what `send`, `send_group`, `send_all` and `text` take.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` on windows (no signal numbers) and on a host wolf
/// has not measured. Never traps. Uncharged (it reads the host's table,
/// as `number`), and refused at comptime all the same. lupin declines by
/// name (it does not read the C library's range), so the example is
/// prose: `signal.last()?` is 64 on linux.
///
/// Delegates to `os_signal_last`.
pub fn last() -> int ! {unsupported} {
    os_signal_last()?
}

/// The C library's real-time signal range (`[os.signal.host]`): host
/// numbers `first` to `last`, both included, or `first` and `last` both 0
/// on a host that has none (macOS). glibc's is 34 to 64 and musl's 35 to
/// 64 on one kernel, which is why a real-time signal is named by its
/// number here and never by a meaning (`RTMIN+3` is `first + 3`).
pub struct Realtime {
    first: int,
    last: int,
}

/// **s241 (`[os.signal.host]`): the real-time range**, as `Realtime`.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` on windows and on a host wolf has not measured.
/// Never traps. Uncharged; refused at comptime. lupin declines by name,
/// so the example is prose: `signal.realtime()?.first` is 34 under glibc.
///
/// Delegates to `os_signal_realtime`.
pub fn realtime() -> Realtime ! {unsupported} {
    let (a, b) = os_signal_realtime()?
    Realtime { first: a, last: b }
}

/// **s241 (`[os.signal.text]`, ruling #79 R5): the host C library's words
/// for `meaning`'s signal**, or for host signal `N` given `host(N)` —
/// `strsignal(3)`, what GNU `kill -t` prints and what a shell says of a
/// job a signal ended. Always in the C locale, whatever `LANG` says. The
/// words are the host's BY DESIGN and differ between C libraries: glibc's
/// `kill()` is "Killed", macOS's "Killed: 9" (it appends the number to
/// all 28); glibc and musl part on `arithmetic()` and `child()` and on
/// every real-time number.
///
/// Rows: `invalid` for 0 or anything `send` refuses, before the host;
/// `unsupported` on windows. Never traps. Uncharged; refused at
/// comptime. lupin answers `invalid` and declines a well-formed call by
/// name (a table there would be one C library's).
///
/// Delegates to `os_signal_text`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let t = signal.text(0) else |_| "invalid"
/// t == "invalid"
/// ```
pub fn text(meaning: int) -> str ! {invalid, unsupported} {
    os_signal_text(meaning)?
}

/// The `meaning` value that names host signal `n` (`[os.signal.host]`,
/// ruling #79 R4): `-n`, `os_wait_status`'s spelling of "ended by signal
/// `n`", so a program re-sends what it saw. For the signals the table
/// gives no meaning — linux `STKFLT`, `IO`, `PWR` and the real-time range;
/// macOS `EMT`, `IO`, `INFO` — and for a number a user typed (`kill -9`).
/// It makes NO portability promise: `n` is this host's number, and for
/// the real-time range this C library's. Only `send`, `send_group`,
/// `send_all` and `text` take one, for `1 <= n <= last()`; every
/// set-taking call refuses it (a negative set fails the one shape rule).
///
/// Total and pure; nothing is checked until a call takes it.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// signal.host(9) == 0 - 9
/// ```
pub fn host(n: int) -> int {
    0 - n
}

/// **s232 (`[os.signal.ignored]`): the set of listenable meanings this
/// process ignores NOW.** It changes nothing. Read before the program's
/// own first `ignore`, `reset` or `listen`, it is what the process was
/// STARTED ignoring — what a shell's `trap` must leave alone and what
/// makes `nohup` work. A listened meaning is not ignored.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` on a host with no dispositions (windows). Never
/// traps. lupin declines by name (no `sigaction` without `unsafe`), so
/// the example is prose: `signal.ignored()? & signal.reload() != 0`
/// asks whether this program runs under `nohup`.
///
/// Capability `env` (ruling #70 R8); refused at comptime (D33).
/// Delegates to `os_signal_ignored`.
pub fn ignored() -> int ! {unsupported} {
    os_signal_ignored()?
}
